    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Subscribe newsletter signups to Mailchimp from a webhook and welcome them by email\n\n**Who's it for**\nAnyone running a newsletter signup form on their own site who wants the subscriber added to\nMailchimp and welcomed immediately, with the form told whether it worked.\n\n**How it works**\nYour signup form posts to the webhook. **`validate the signup`** checks the body before anything\nelse happens, and an invalid payload is answered with an error response rather than being allowed\nto reach Mailchimp. A valid signup is looked up first: an address already on the list is updated\nrather than re-added, so a repeat signup refreshes the record instead of creating a duplicate or\nfailing. Either way the subscriber gets a welcome email, and the form receives a success response.\nBoth outcomes end in an explicit response node, so the browser is never left waiting.\n\n**How to set up**\n1. Open **`config`** and set your Mailchimp audience id and the welcome email subject.\n2. Attach Mailchimp and Gmail credentials to the `[cred]` nodes.\n3. Copy the production URL of **`When someone subscribes`** into your form's action or fetch call.\n4. Post a test signup and check the response body.\n\n**Requirements**\nA Mailchimp account with an audience, and a Gmail account for the welcome email.\n\n**How to customize**\nThe welcome email is built in **`[cred] Gmail - send the welcome email`** — swap that node for any\nother sender without touching the rest. To capture extra fields, add them in\n**`validate the signup`** and pass them through as merge fields.",
        "height": 972,
        "width": 640,
        "color": 4
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        0,
        -1312
      ],
      "id": "sticky-00",
      "name": "Template overview"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "### 1. Validate before anything else\n\n**`config`** holds the audience id and the welcome subject in one place.\n\n**`validate the signup`** checks the posted body *before* any Mailchimp call. A malformed or empty payload is answered with an error, so a broken form never spends an API call or writes a junk record.",
        "height": 202,
        "width": 1040,
        "color": 7
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        0,
        -242
      ],
      "id": "sticky-01",
      "name": "1. Validate before anything else"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "### 2. Find first, then add or update\n\nThe lookup is what makes this safe to call twice. An address already on the list takes the **update** branch; a new one takes **add**.\n\nWithout it, a returning subscriber either creates a duplicate or fails the run — and people do resubmit forms.",
        "height": 268,
        "width": 480,
        "color": 3
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        1120,
        330
      ],
      "id": "sticky-02",
      "name": "2. Find first, then add or update"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "### 3. Always answer the form\n\nBoth paths end in an explicit response node — one for the rejected signup, one for the successful one.\n\nA webhook that ends without responding leaves the visitor's browser waiting until it times out, which looks like a broken site even though the subscriber was added.",
        "height": 290,
        "width": 480,
        "color": 7
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        1680,
        -330
      ],
      "id": "sticky-03",
      "name": "3. Always answer the form"
    }
  ],
